     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. A medication order has to come from a licensed prescriber.** Order entry records a prescribing decision, and it must be tied to a clinician who can be held accountable for it. Since you're not a clinician, the patient's treating physician (or another licensed prescriber on the medicine team) needs to place the order under their own name. If I entered it on your request, it would bypass the authorization and accountability that the system depends on.\n\n**2. The dose is unsafe as written.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to **5100 mg/day**. That's about twice the usual maximum:\n- Immediate-release: max 2550 mg/day (for example, 850 mg three times daily)\n- Extended-release: max 2000 mg/day\n\nAt that dose, the risks of serious GI problems and lactic acidosis go up. A clinician would need to review this even if the order came from a prescriber.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Pass the request to the patient's attending or the medicine team so they can review it and enter it themselves.\n- It's possible the intended order was 850 mg three times daily (2550 mg/day) or 1700 mg total per day, and something got mixed up when it was passed along. The prescriber can confirm.\n- If you need help with administrative or operational tasks, I'm happy to assist with those."
      }
     ],
